# 6. Dose-response

Adapted for BioLang from Avril Coghlan's [CC BY 3.0 booklet](https://a-little-book-of-r-for-biomedical-statistics.readthedocs.io/en/latest/). The lesson adds a visual trend check and separates the descriptive odds ratios from the model assumption.

Increasing outcome odds with increasing dose is a useful clue. It is not automatically linear, causal, or free from confounding.

> **Understanding dose-response trends**
>
> **Use them when:** exposure has a meaningful ordered level and you want to test whether outcome frequency changes systematically across it.
>
> **How to read them:** plot every dose group before fitting a line. A trend coefficient summarises one assumed shape; curvature, unequal group sizes, and influential endpoints can make that summary misleading.

```biolang
let doses = [2.0, 9.5, 19.5, 37.0, 50.0]
let disease = [35.0, 250.0, 196.0, 136.0, 32.0]
let controls = [82.0, 293.0, 190.0, 71.0, 13.0]

let log_odds = [
    log(disease[0] / controls[0]),
    log(disease[1] / controls[1]),
    log(disease[2] / controls[2]),
    log(disease[3] / controls[3]),
    log(disease[4] / controls[4])
]

let baseline_odds = disease[0] / controls[0]
let dose_odds_ratios = [
    (disease[0] / controls[0]) / baseline_odds,
    (disease[1] / controls[1]) / baseline_odds,
    (disease[2] / controls[2]) / baseline_odds,
    (disease[3] / controls[3]) / baseline_odds,
    (disease[4] / controls[4]) / baseline_odds
]
table({dose: doses, disease: disease, control: controls, odds_ratio_vs_lowest: dose_odds_ratios, log_odds: log_odds})
```

The ORs rise from 1 to about 2.00, 2.42, 4.49, and 5.77. Plot the underlying log odds rather than only the fitted line.

```biolang
stats_relationship_plot(doses, log_odds, {
    format: "svg",
    title: "Dose and observed log odds",
    x_label: "Dose",
    y_label: "log(disease / control)"
})
```

```biolang
let trend = lm(doses, log_odds)
trend
```

The slope test gives p ≈ 0.00659 for this simple linear model, matching the source calculation. Before trusting it, inspect curvature, influential dose groups, group sizes, uncertainty, and whether dose was assigned or merely observed.
